WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:04.280
When you're traveling by air, you want to be
sitting in a comfortable seat.

00:00:04.310 --> 00:00:10.520
Private aircraft take comfort to a level far
above that of commercial airlines with seats

00:00:10.520 --> 00:00:13.250
that are as cozy and luxurious as fine
furniture.

00:00:14.090 --> 00:00:16.550
They must also be lightweight and fire
resistant.

00:00:20.110 --> 00:00:22.300
On private and corporate aircraft.

00:00:22.300 --> 00:00:24.460
It's all about luxury seating.

00:00:24.490 --> 00:00:30.460
Armchairs often swivel, and at the push of a
button recline with an extending footrest.

00:00:30.490 --> 00:00:36.040
The cushions are constructed from layers of
fireproof foam containing carbon fiber for

00:00:36.040 --> 00:00:38.230
optimum support and durability.

00:00:38.380 --> 00:00:43.240
Workers cut the cushion pieces using a
bandsaw with a round blade that slices

00:00:43.240 --> 00:00:45.520
through foam without tearing or shredding
it.

00:00:46.030 --> 00:00:51.310
They spray adjoining surfaces with water
based contact cement and assemble them.

00:00:51.910 --> 00:00:56.740
The assembly combines different thicknesses
in three different densities to make the

00:00:56.770 --> 00:00:59.560
cushions soft in certain areas and rigid in
others.

00:01:00.550 --> 00:01:06.590
The densities are color coded after letting
the glue dry for 12 hours.

00:01:06.610 --> 00:01:08.840
Workers begin the final shaping.

00:01:08.860 --> 00:01:11.370
This is the seat cushion assembly.

00:01:11.380 --> 00:01:15.460
They first saw through it laterally to make
it the right thickness.

00:01:15.940 --> 00:01:21.290
Then they temporarily adhere a plastic
template of the final cushion shape trace it

00:01:21.290 --> 00:01:23.390
then saw along the trace line.

00:01:23.990 --> 00:01:29.450
Next, using the technical drawing as a
guide, they mark then grind the cushions

00:01:29.450 --> 00:01:34.260
rounded profile, holding the next layer of
foam over the edge.

00:01:34.280 --> 00:01:36.890
They verify the shape with another template.

00:01:37.970 --> 00:01:44.070
Then they mark lines for the cushions,
creases and with a foam saw, cut them five

00:01:44.070 --> 00:01:47.970
centimetres deep, just enough to insert
velcro strips.

00:01:47.990 --> 00:01:53.030
The backsides of which they glue to the foam
with water based contact cement.

00:01:55.010 --> 00:01:59.130
Workers construct the backrest cushion just
like the seat cushion.

00:01:59.150 --> 00:02:02.930
Assembling foam pieces of different
thicknesses and densities.

00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:04.460
Shaping the perimeter.

00:02:04.490 --> 00:02:05.540
Grinding the edge.

00:02:05.540 --> 00:02:08.720
Profile. Cutting and Velcro increases.

00:02:09.950 --> 00:02:16.940
Then they apply a final layer of soft foam
13mm thick, folding the edges onto the Velcro

00:02:16.940 --> 00:02:18.410
inside the creases.

00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:23.150
They do the same with the seat cushion,
armrests and footrest.

00:02:24.710 --> 00:02:29.990
All the foam cushions are now ready to be
covered in genuine leather in accordance with

00:02:29.990 --> 00:02:31.370
safety regulations.

00:02:31.370 --> 00:02:37.310
The supplier rendered the leather fireproof
by treating it with a calcium based solution.

00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:43.550
Seamstresses trace patterns for the cover
pieces, then cut them out.

00:02:46.010 --> 00:02:54.620
They line the perimeter of each piece with
double faced tape, fold over the edges to

00:02:54.620 --> 00:02:59.900
create a neat hem and trim the corners flat.

00:03:02.030 --> 00:03:04.370
Then they sew on Velcro strips where
required.

00:03:08.120 --> 00:03:13.760
Finally, they assemble the pieces into
covers, sewing an identification label in

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:23.720
each one. They hammer every seam flat to
prevent bulges.

00:03:23.990 --> 00:03:30.260
Then they So what's called a French stitch,
a line of straight stitches on either side of

00:03:30.260 --> 00:03:36.560
each seam. Meanwhile, workers glue fireproof
polyester batting to the foam.

00:03:36.680 --> 00:03:41.120
This layer between foam and leather will
even out the surface.

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:47.270
They tear the perimeter of the batting to
thin it out.

00:03:49.010 --> 00:03:52.820
Finally, it's time to dress the cushions in
their covers.

00:03:52.910 --> 00:03:58.100
Workers use a metal rod to help push the
leather deep into the creases without

00:03:58.100 --> 00:04:03.470
bunching up. Velcro strips on the cover
adhere to the velcro strips inside the

00:04:03.470 --> 00:04:09.800
creases. The covers also have velcro
closures and certain cushions have velcro

00:04:09.800 --> 00:04:13.070
strips on the bottom which attach to the
seat structure.

00:04:13.970 --> 00:04:18.140
Once all the covers are on, workers can
complete the seat assembly.

00:04:18.350 --> 00:04:25.250
They attach the armrests to the structure
with screws, then install the footrest which

00:04:25.280 --> 00:04:26.780
attaches with velcro.

00:04:26.990 --> 00:04:29.570
It contains a cavity for storing the life
vest.

00:04:32.990 --> 00:04:35.960
The seat cushion also attaches with velcro.

00:04:40.010 --> 00:04:45.230
The passenger seats and pilot seat are
constructed the same way, except that the

00:04:45.230 --> 00:04:51.290
pilot seat often has a sheepskin insert on
the sitting surface to absorb sweat and keep

00:04:51.290 --> 00:04:53.540
the pilot cool and comfortable.

00:04:57.350 --> 00:05:01.820
It was the ancient Romans who first began
using cremation urns.

00:05:01.850 --> 00:05:04.010
Today, the custom continues.

00:05:04.130 --> 00:05:09.920
The remains are stored in urns permanently
or sometimes temporarily, before burial.

00:05:09.950 --> 00:05:15.560
And with cremation, one's final resting
place doesn't have to be six feet under.

00:05:19.150 --> 00:05:22.570
The cremation urn has an important place in
some households.

00:05:23.170 --> 00:05:28.450
It's a constant reminder of the dearly
departed helping to keep the memories alive.

00:05:31.120 --> 00:05:33.460
The urn starts with a concept.

00:05:33.910 --> 00:05:38.920
The potter draws a large scale design, which
he'll use as a reference as he shapes a

00:05:38.920 --> 00:05:40.750
prototype urn from clay.

00:05:42.400 --> 00:05:44.890
He slaps a lump of clay onto the potter's
wheel.

00:05:45.610 --> 00:05:50.740
The wheel spins and the potter begins to
transform the shapeless lump into the desired

00:05:50.740 --> 00:05:59.230
form. The process is called throwing a
potter's term for shaping.

00:06:00.070 --> 00:06:05.680
He leaves the bottom open so the walls can
be more easily formed into the desired shape.

00:06:06.640 --> 00:06:12.730
He'll add a base to the urn later as he pulls
up the clay to produce the walls.

00:06:12.730 --> 00:06:15.430
He works it to a uniform thickness.

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:20.800
He gathers the top to narrow the opening, a
technique known as collaring.

00:06:24.140 --> 00:06:30.170
Then with the potter's wheel at a stop, he
takes the shape from round to rectangular and

00:06:30.170 --> 00:06:31.550
tapers it at the neck.

00:06:33.050 --> 00:06:38.150
Then it's into a casting box which exposes
only half of the urn prototype.

00:06:38.180 --> 00:06:42.900
He brushes a release agent onto that half
using a mixer.

00:06:42.920 --> 00:06:45.620
He whips up a mix of plaster and water.

00:06:46.040 --> 00:06:50.300
He blends it until it thickens to the
consistency of pancake batter.

00:06:50.690 --> 00:06:56.150
The liquefied plaster will harden in
minutes, so he quickly pours it into the box

00:06:56.150 --> 00:06:59.540
with the urn prototype and fills it right to
the top.

00:07:01.040 --> 00:07:06.710
As the plaster hardens, the exposed urn
prototype makes an impression of half of the

00:07:06.710 --> 00:07:11.600
urn. He'll make another one exactly like it
for the other half of the urn.

00:07:16.250 --> 00:07:18.980
He'll attach them with a key system for
this.

00:07:19.460 --> 00:07:22.100
He drills notches in one mold half.

00:07:25.160 --> 00:07:29.900
The notches match up with raised areas on
the other mold half to align them.

00:07:33.160 --> 00:07:38.680
He ties the molds together with suspender
like straps, cinching them tightly together.

00:07:41.050 --> 00:07:45.220
He adds a base to the urn and straps it to
the other two pieces.

00:07:46.030 --> 00:07:50.710
He sets the assembled mold upright, exposing
the opening of the urn.

00:07:52.090 --> 00:07:55.270
He's now ready to make a copy of the
original urn.

00:07:55.300 --> 00:08:00.190
In fact, he'll make 20 at once using 20
identical plaster molds.

00:08:00.370 --> 00:08:06.290
He pumps liquid clay into each one, filling
it to the brim over 3 to 4 hours.

00:08:06.310 --> 00:08:08.650
The plaster absorbs water in the clay.

00:08:08.650 --> 00:08:10.930
It comes into contact with causing it to
solidify.

00:08:11.890 --> 00:08:14.530
The clay in the center remains liquid.

00:08:15.070 --> 00:08:18.970
He pours that into a barrel to be strained
and reused.

00:08:22.120 --> 00:08:24.250
And now the big reveal.

00:08:24.400 --> 00:08:27.430
He opens the mold and removes the urn.

00:08:27.460 --> 00:08:30.760
It's exactly like the prototype sculpted by
the potter.

00:08:34.150 --> 00:08:38.710
He lifts the lid from another plastic mold
and checks the fit to the urn.

00:08:42.260 --> 00:08:48.500
After drying and an initial firing to harden
the clay, a worker coats the urn and the lid

00:08:48.500 --> 00:08:49.560
with a glaze.

00:08:49.580 --> 00:08:51.740
Working from the inside out.

00:08:53.250 --> 00:08:57.000
The glaze is a mixture of finely milled
minerals and water.

00:08:57.030 --> 00:08:59.760
It adds color and a glass like non-porous
surface.

00:09:00.360 --> 00:09:02.310
When fired a second time.

00:09:03.210 --> 00:09:06.210
Cremation urns come in a range of styles and
colors.

00:09:06.960 --> 00:09:11.430
Customers often make a selection that
reflects the taste of the deceased.

00:09:13.280 --> 00:09:18.440
Another worker now brushes a mixture of wax
and alumina onto the neck of the urn and the

00:09:18.440 --> 00:09:22.940
lid. This will keep them from fusing
together during the final firing.

00:09:25.150 --> 00:09:32.800
They fire the cremation urns at a very
intense 1195°C for about 15 hours.

00:09:32.830 --> 00:09:35.560
This transforms the clay into a durable
ceramic.

00:09:38.180 --> 00:09:42.860
They call the urns slowly for 13 hours to
prevent cracking.

00:09:44.150 --> 00:09:45.830
Four days in the making.

00:09:45.830 --> 00:09:52.340
These urns are now ready to contain a loved
one's remains and in so doing, honor a life.

